====================================================================
DOMAIN: Dussel and Postcolonial/Subaltern Critics
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-04-30
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 6, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, PhilPapers (cached)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain situates Dussel's account of Eurocentrism within the broader
constellation of postcolonial theory (Said, Spivak, Bhabha), South Asian
subaltern studies, and African/Caribbean critiques (Wynter, Mbembe,
Mudimbe, Fanon, Cesaire). The defining axis of debate is the
postcolonial/decolonial distinction articulated by figures such as
Mignolo, Grosfoguel, and Maldonado-Torres: where postcolonialism centers
nineteenth-century British and French empire and Foucauldian/discourse
analysis, the decolonial school anchored by Dussel and Quijano centers
1492 and the Iberian conquest, and theorizes the modernity/coloniality
nexus in world-systems terms inherited from Wallerstein.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
These materials supply the comparative frame for assessing Dussel's
distinctiveness: his 1492-centered narrative, his refusal of the
linguistic turn's primacy, and his theological-Marxian commitments
diverge from Said's Foucauldian Orientalism critique and Spivak's
deconstructive subalternity, while Caribbean and African critics
(Cesaire, Wynter, Ndlovu-Gatsheni) sometimes converge with and sometimes
press back against his Latin Americanist starting point.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct sustained engagements between Dussel and Said, or Dussel and
Spivak, are rare in the cached results; most comparison runs through
intermediaries such as Mignolo and Grosfoguel. African critiques of
Dussel (as opposed to parallel African decolonial work) are also
under-represented. Several entries lack abstracts (marked INCOMPLETE
where applicable).

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Use Lindner (2025) and Grosfoguel (2011) as the central pivot for the
postcolonial vs decolonial distinction; pair Maldonado-Torres (2011) and
Bhambra (2014) to articulate convergence; bring in Ndlovu-Gatsheni (2020)
and Vellem (2017) for the African inflection; use Lange (1998) and the
Morana/Dussel/Jauregui volume (2008) for internal critiques of Dussel.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Decolonial/Latin Americanist (1492 origin): 5 papers
- Postcolonial/Anglophone (19th-c. empire, discourse): 2 papers
- Convergence and dialogue: 4 papers
- Internal feminist/critical revision of Dussel: 2 papers
====================================================================
}

@article{lindner2025provincializing,
  author = {Lindner, Kolja},
  title = {Provincializing or Delinking},
  journal = {Interdisciplinary Review of ValEUs},
  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.5195/valeus.2025.8},
  note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Distinguishes postcolonialism's strategy of provincializing Europe (Chakrabarty) from decolonialism's delinking from Western epistemology (Mignolo, Quijano), tracing how each approach handles Eurocentrism and reading Fanon and C.L.R. James through both lenses. RELEVANCE: Provides the central framing question of this domain, namely the postcolonial/decolonial distinction in which Dussel is a paradigmatic decolonial figure. POSITION: Postcolonial vs decolonial debate, arguing the postcolonial framework better accommodates James and Fanon than the decolonial.},
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial, Eurocentrism, Chakrabarty, Mignolo, High}
}

